	<h1>The Open Ops Manual</h1>
	<p>
		<strong>Mission:</strong> Disintermediation — removing the parasitic middlemen from digital and physical infrastructure to empower community sovereignty.
	</p>
	<p>
		At Punk Positronics, the process of building a business should be as open as the software used to run it. This is the "Open Ops" commitment: documenting struggles, wins, and systems in real time.
	</p>
	<p>
		By open-sourcing the "How-To" of operations, we build trust and attract a tribe of like-minded creators and entrepreneurs who want to reclaim sovereignty over their own infrastructure.
	</p>

	<h2>Core Ethos</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>A man, not a corporation:</strong> people & planet first.</li>
		<li><strong>Themes:</strong> FOSS, SolarPunk, Maker Culture, Upcycling, Hardware Reclamation, Self-Hosting, DIY, and a Homesteader mentality.</li>
		<li><strong>Radical transparency:</strong> if we find a better way to do things, we share it.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Hardware Policy — Teach, Don't Vend</h2>
	<p>
		No inventory. No hardware sales. No warranties on gear. Empowerment means the client's own device, the client's own hands, guided by expertise. Our reclaimed devices — the Demo Phone, the salvage laptops — exist as examples and proof of what's possible, never as product. The moment we resell gear, we become the middleman with a markup. The business stays pure service: knowledge, labor, and guidance.
	</p>

	<h2>Energy Transparency</h2>
	<p>
		The operation is committed to renewable energy suppliers — and to documenting the struggle, failures, and compromises of sourcing green power in a corporate energy landscape. Salvage enterprise iron on a renewable tariff is Energy Transparency made literal: the greenest computer is the one that already exists.
	</p>

	<h2>The Succession Protocol</h2>
	<p>
		This business is being built as a community-resilient asset. By open-sourcing the operational manual, we ensure it can be maintained or resumed by the community — removing "the man" as a single point of failure. Documentation beyond SOPs: a blueprint for business resilience, so the work outlives the operator.
	</p>

	<h2>The Solo Operating Model</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Fulfillment is the spine;</strong> consulting runs as capped quarterly sprints — two Cloud Exit Audits a month, max. No bespoke one-offs in year one.</li>
		<li><strong>The Guardian Retainer is VIP-only:</strong> 3–5 clients, premium pricing, application-only. The SLA is defined in writing — scheduled maintenance windows, not on-call heroics.</li>
		<li><strong>Education is exhaust, not a workstream:</strong> the Manual documents work already in progress; Masterclasses and Build-Days stay parked until there's a waitlist.</li>
		<li><strong>Capacity ceilings are commitments:</strong> past the ceiling there's a waitlist, not overtime. Batch by day type — Fulfillment Days, Consulting Days, Admin + Content Days. Context-switching is the real tax.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Financial Sovereignty</h2>
	<p>
		Removing the parasitic middlemen from the flow of value, too. The Hub: primary accounts at a local credit union. The Edge: a low-fee fintech layer for transactional efficiency. Community funding first via direct payment links; future transition to Open Collective for radical, community-led transparency.
	</p>

	<h2>The Honest Ledger</h2>
	<p>
		Known risks, stated in public: the audience bet is the one pillar with no 15-year track record behind it. Logistics, automation, documentation, and infrastructure all have receipts — audience-building is the skill to over-invest in early. That's why the library table exists: buying those reps cheaply, in public, before any infrastructure spend.
	</p>

	<h2>The Roadmap: Shed → Hub</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>Phase 0 — The Library Table (now):</strong> flat-fee Quick Wins at the local library, no infrastructure required. Two client slots a month, weekends only. Substack posts start here.</li>
		<li><strong>Phase 1 — Prototype:</strong> build the NAS, onboard 1–3 beta authors, document the build in public.</li>
		<li><strong>Phase 2 — Lean Operator:</strong> capped Guardian Retainers, then Build-Day workshops when the waitlist demands it.</li>
		<li><strong>Phase 3 — Community Hub:</strong> a local studio and part-time maker-space. De-risked by experience — the corporate facility relocation this operator personally managed makes Shed → Hub a known quantity.</li>
	</ol>
